WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.112
For a long time, forgetting was a natural given of existence. A youthful mistake, an unfortunate remark, an embarrassing photograph eventually faded from memory, for lack of traces. The Internet has overturned that balance: now, what has been published once can resurface years later, out of context, thanks to a simple search.

00:00:22.362 --> 00:00:46.557
It was to address this problem that the Court of Justice of the European Union established, in 2014, what is now called the right to delisting: an individual may ask a search engine to stop linking their name to information that is inadequate, irrelevant or no longer relevant, or excessive. The information itself is not deleted; it simply becomes harder to find.

00:00:46.807 --> 00:01:11.848
Yet this right is not self-evident. Its critics see it as a threat to freedom of expression and to the work of historians: who will decide what deserves to be forgotten? Could a politician make an old scandal vanish on the grounds that it is old? To prevent such abuses, requests are examined case by case, weighing respect for private life against the public's interest in access to the information.

00:01:12.098 --> 00:01:42.022
The European data-protection regulation, in force since 2018, has since written a 'right to erasure' into its Article 17. One major limit remains: in 2019 the same Court ruled that a search engine was not obliged to apply delisting beyond the European versions of its service. Information removed from the results in Paris can therefore remain accessible from New York — a reminder that the law often stops at borders the network ignores.

00:01:42.272 --> 00:02:11.814
At bottom, the debate goes beyond technology. It raises a question every society must settle: do we have the right to change, and to have others recognise it? A democracy that forgot nothing would risk condemning everyone to remain a prisoner of their worst self. A democracy that forgot too easily, on the other hand, would lose the memory of what happened to it. Between these two pitfalls, the right to be forgotten is neither a miracle cure nor censorship in disguise, but an ever-fragile compromise.
